But the meaning of the sentence is undifferentiated [between ordinary and Vedic usage]. // MS_1,2.40 // {*1/530*}
The meaning of words used in ordinary life and in the <s>Veda</s> is undifferentiated. Just as [meaning] is intended in ordinary life, so too it ought to be in the <s>Veda</s>. [Objection:] This is not so — in ordinary life, communication occurs when meanings are understood. But here, with deities who are imperceptible [59]{*1/531*} and ritual implements that are insentient, conversation serves no purpose for the sacrifice. If a transcendent result (<s>adṛṣṭa</s>) were postulated, it ought to arise from the mere utterance. For what must be done is purposeful, and utterance must in any case be done — whether for a transcendent purpose or for a meaning-purpose. If the meaning is not conveyed, nothing is purposeless. If [the mantra] is not applied [ritually], then the recording [in the Veda] would be purposeless. Therefore, from the utterance [arises] the transcendent result. And thus it has been stated from the treatise on that purpose {*1/532*}.
To this it is said: The utterance of mantras in sacrifice is indeed for the purpose of conveying meaning. As for what was said — 'there is no purpose in conversation with deities and ritual implements' — the purpose in sacrifice is precisely the illumination of the sacrifice and its components. How? For without the sacrifice and its components being illuminated [i.e., identified], the sacrifice cannot be performed. Therefore, the illumination of meaning for the accomplishment [of the sacrifice] is a great assistance to the rite, and that [illumination] is accomplished [by the mantra] — this is understood. Therefore, the purpose [served is that] of what does not [otherwise] exist [in the sacrificer's awareness]. And this visible [purpose] cannot be denied — [namely, that] the expression of meaning is the purpose. Even if, in [cases where] the expression of meaning is [already] helpful — [mantras like] 'he takes it with four [verses]' and the like — let them be purposeless {*1/533*}; [still,] we would never deny the visible benefit of meaning-expression.

